{
  "sections": [
    {
      "role": "QUAL",
      "opinions": [
        {
          "statement": "The mathematical formulation of the orthogonalization step is technically sound, correctly identifying the nearest orthonormal matrix via the SVD of the gradient matrix.",
          "source_roles": [
            "QUAL"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-004"
          ],
          "suggestions": []
        },
        {
          "statement": "The experimental evaluation lacks comparisons against modern state-of-the-art optimizers (e.g., Adam, LAMB), limiting the assessment of the method's practical utility in contemporary deep learning workflows.",
          "source_roles": [
            "QUAL"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-005",
            "SRC-008"
          ],
          "suggestions": [
            "Include comparisons with at least one modern adaptive optimizer (e.g., AdamW) to demonstrate if the speed-up persists against more sophisticated baselines than SGDM."
          ]
        },
        {
          "statement": "While standard errors are reported for CIFAR-10 results, the ImageNet and Barlow Twins experiments appear to rely on single runs or lack explicit variance reporting, weakening the statistical rigor of the claims regarding large-scale performance.",
          "source_roles": [
            "QUAL"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-008"
          ],
          "suggestions": [
            "Report mean and standard deviation over multiple independent runs for the ImageNet and Barlow Twins experiments to ensure statistical significance."
          ]
        },
        {
          "statement": "The authors provide code links for both the optimizer implementation and the experiments, and specify key hyperparameters (learning rate, momentum, weight decay) in the text, facilitating reproducibility.",
          "source_roles": [
            "QUAL"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-004",
            "SRC-005"
          ],
          "suggestions": []
        },
        {
          "statement": "The comparison to prior work is limited to SGDM and the original ResNet hyperparameters; the paper does not sufficiently compare against other methods that address feature redundancy or diversity (e.g., orthogonal weight constraints, or other diversity-promoting regularizers).",
          "source_roles": [
            "QUAL"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-002",
            "SRC-007"
          ],
          "suggestions": [
            "Discuss and compare against other techniques that aim to diversify learned representations or enforce orthogonality in weights/gradients to better position the contribution."
          ]
        },
        {
          "statement": "The paper acknowledges that the method fails to match SGDM performance when using SGDM-tuned hyperparameters (Table 2), but does not provide a thorough ablation or analysis on why the orthogonalization interferes with the specific learning rate schedule or weight decay settings used in the tuned baseline.",
          "source_roles": [
            "QUAL"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-007"
          ],
          "suggestions": [
            "Provide a more detailed analysis of the interaction between orthogonalization and specific hyperparameters (e.g., learning rate decay) to explain the performance gap in Table 2."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "role": "CLAR",
      "opinions": [
        {
          "statement": "The paper contains several grammatical errors and typos that reduce readability and professionalism, such as 'SPEEDUP' in the title and 'filers' in Section 3.1.",
          "source_roles": [
            "CLAR"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-001",
            "SRC-009"
          ],
          "suggestions": [
            "Correct the title typo 'SPEEDUP' to 'SPEED UP' or 'ACCELERATE', fix the typo 'filers' to 'filters' in Section 3.1, and review the entire manuscript for grammatical consistency."
          ]
        },
        {
          "statement": "The mathematical notation for the network architecture and component definitions is dense and potentially confusing, particularly regarding the mapping dimensions and the relationship between $c_l$ and $f_l$.",
          "source_roles": [
            "CLAR"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-003"
          ],
          "suggestions": [
            "Clarify the relationship between $c_l$ and $f_l$ and ensure the dimensions of the gradient matrix $G_l$ ($P_l \\times N_l$) are explicitly linked to the parameterization $\\theta_l$ in Section 2.2."
          ]
        },
        {
          "statement": "The algorithm presentation lacks a clear pseudocode block, relying instead on inline equations which makes the step-by-step procedure less immediately accessible.",
          "source_roles": [
            "CLAR"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-004"
          ],
          "suggestions": [
            "Include a formal Algorithm 1 box that summarizes the Orthogonal-SGDM update rule, explicitly listing inputs, outputs, and detailing the SVD step."
          ]
        },
        {
          "statement": "Figure captions are sometimes insufficiently descriptive, requiring the reader to cross-reference the main text to understand the specific experimental setup or model layer being shown.",
          "source_roles": [
            "CLAR"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-010"
          ],
          "suggestions": [
            "Expand figure captions to include key experimental details (e.g., model name, dataset, specific layer index) directly in the caption to ensure self-contained understanding."
          ]
        },
        {
          "statement": "The paper is generally well-organized with a logical flow from hypothesis to method to results to discussion.",
          "source_roles": [
            "CLAR"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-002"
          ],
          "suggestions": []
        },
        {
          "statement": "The notation for the cosine similarity sets $C_l$ and $R_l$ uses absolute values of inner products, which is non-standard for cosine similarity (usually normalized), potentially making the term 'cosine similarity' misleading if vectors are not normalized.",
          "source_roles": [
            "CLAR"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-009"
          ],
          "suggestions": [
            "Clarify whether $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle_2$ denotes the dot product or the cosine similarity, and define the normalization step explicitly if applicable."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "role": "SIGN",
      "opinions": [
        {
          "statement": "The work addresses a broadly important problem in neural network optimization by targeting the inefficiency of redundant component learning, a challenge relevant to a wide range of deep learning architectures.",
          "source_roles": [
            "SIGN"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-002"
          ],
          "suggestions": []
        },
        {
          "statement": "The method demonstrates domain impact by showing consistent improvements across a suite of complex architectures (ResNets, DenseNets) on standard computer vision benchmarks (CIFAR-10, ImageNet), indicating generalizability beyond simple models.",
          "source_roles": [
            "SIGN"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-005",
            "SRC-008"
          ],
          "suggestions": []
        },
        {
          "statement": "The method does not improve upon the state-of-the-art in terms of final accuracy; when using hyperparameters tuned for SGDM, Orthogonal-SGDM underperforms SGDM (90.18% vs 91.17% on CIFAR-10 ResNet20), and the authors explicitly state that tuned SGDM remains superior.",
          "source_roles": [
            "SIGN"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-007"
          ],
          "suggestions": [
            "Clarify the specific niche where this method is preferred over tuned SGDM, as the current evidence suggests it is a trade-off for speed/robustness rather than a SOTA improvement in performance."
          ]
        },
        {
          "statement": "The paper lacks a thorough discussion of the practical societal and computational impact, specifically noting that the SVD operation introduces non-linear time complexity and can be 'prohibitively expensive' for dense layers, without providing a clear mitigation strategy or cost-benefit analysis for large-scale deployment.",
          "source_roles": [
            "SIGN"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-011"
          ],
          "suggestions": [
            "Include a more detailed analysis of the wall-time overhead versus convergence speed-up to help practitioners assess the net computational benefit, especially for large-scale models."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "role": "ORIG",
      "opinions": [
        {
          "statement": "The paper introduces a novel optimization algorithm, Orthogonal-SGDM, which applies SVD-based orthogonalization to the gradient matrix of layer components, representing a distinct algorithmic contribution that modifies the update rule of first-order optimizers to enforce diversity in learned representations.",
          "source_roles": [
            "ORIG"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-004"
          ],
          "suggestions": []
        },
        {
          "statement": "The problem formulation highlights a specific inefficiency in neural network training where components in a layer co-learn similar representations initially, wasting updates, providing a clear motivation for the proposed method.",
          "source_roles": [
            "ORIG"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-002",
            "SRC-003"
          ],
          "suggestions": []
        },
        {
          "statement": "The analysis of the hypothesis regarding co-learning is inconclusive and somewhat contradictory, as the paper reports that Orthogonal-SGDM actually results in more similar component parameters (higher cosine similarity) than SGDM, which contradicts the initial hypothesis that orthogonalization prevents co-learning, leaving the mechanism of the speedup unclear.",
          "source_roles": [
            "ORIG"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-009"
          ],
          "suggestions": [
            "Provide a more rigorous theoretical or empirical analysis to explain why orthogonalizing gradients leads to faster convergence despite the observed increase in parameter similarity, and clarify the relationship between gradient orthogonality and final weight diversity."
          ]
        },
        {
          "statement": "The experimental setup lacks a fair comparison of wall-clock time; the paper claims a 'speedup' in learning but acknowledges that the SVD computation adds significant overhead, making the claim misleading as it only refers to the number of iterations/epochs required to reach a certain accuracy, not the actual time taken.",
          "source_roles": [
            "ORIG"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-011"
          ],
          "suggestions": [
            "Include wall-clock time comparisons between SGDM and Orthogonal-SGDM to substantiate the claim of 'speedup', as the SVD overhead may negate the iteration savings."
          ]
        },
        {
          "statement": "The combination of SVD-based orthogonalization with SGDM is a creative application of linear algebra tools to optimization, though the rationale for choosing SVD over other orthogonalization methods (like QR) is based on standard numerical stability considerations for rank-deficient gradients rather than a novel insight.",
          "source_roles": [
            "ORIG"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-011"
          ],
          "suggestions": []
        },
        {
          "statement": "The paper provides valuable negative results and limitations, such as the performance degradation with small mini-batch sizes and the inability to match state-of-the-art results when using hyperparameters tuned for SGDM, which are important for understanding the method's practical constraints.",
          "source_roles": [
            "ORIG"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-007",
            "SRC-013"
          ],
          "suggestions": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "role": "POL",
      "opinions": [
        {
          "statement": "The paper adheres to double-blind review policies by using 'Anonymous authors' and 'Paper under double-blind review' in the header, and by providing anonymized code repositories via anonymous.4open.science.",
          "source_roles": [
            "POL"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-001",
            "SRC-004"
          ],
          "suggestions": []
        },
        {
          "statement": "The datasets used (CIFAR-10, ImageNet) are standard, publicly available benchmarks that do not contain sensitive personally identifiable information (PII) or require specific privacy compliance measures beyond standard academic usage.",
          "source_roles": [
            "POL"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-005",
            "SRC-008"
          ],
          "suggestions": []
        },
        {
          "statement": "The paper lacks a dedicated 'Broader Impact' or 'Ethical Considerations' section, which is a standard requirement for ICLR submissions to discuss societal implications, potential harms, or responsible AI practices.",
          "source_roles": [
            "POL"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-013"
          ],
          "suggestions": [
            "Add a concise Broader Impact statement discussing the societal benefits of faster training (e.g., reduced computational cost) and any potential risks or limitations regarding the generalizability of the method to safety-critical applications."
          ]
        },
        {
          "statement": "The work is a methodological contribution to optimization and does not present immediate dual-use risks or ethical concerns regarding bias or harm to marginalized populations, as it does not generate content or make decisions directly.",
          "source_roles": [
            "POL"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-002"
          ],
          "suggestions": []
        },
        {
          "statement": "The paper cites relevant prior work (e.g., Biehl et al., He et al., Zbontar et al.) and presents a novel optimization technique, with no evidence of plagiarism or dual submission.",
          "source_roles": [
            "POL"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-003",
            "SRC-014"
          ],
          "suggestions": []
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "recommendation": "Weak Accept"
}

